  "final_text": "All four steps are done. The dashboard has two panels, and the alert rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' has the time field `timestamp`. Other fields include `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `Dest`, `DestCityName`, `FlightDelay`, and `AvgTicketPrice`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-80492326 Flights' (id `s4-80492326-flights-0efefe5dd294`) has two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count, date-histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Flights by carrier\": a bar chart of count, split by terms on `Carrier`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-80492326 Cancelled flights' (id `38c0dd03-298c-440b-9ebf-a1f49d401505`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights`, running every 1m. It counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over a 5-minute window on `timestamp` and fires when the count is above 10. It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n4. **Panel edit:** The carrier panel is now titled \"Flights by carrier\". I renamed it with `update_panel`, which replaces the panel's spec, so I kept the same bar/count/Carrier configuration. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` (status `ok`).\n\nChoices I made: the carrier panel uses a bar chart rather than a pie, and the rule's query is a `term` filter on `Cancelled: true` with a `size` of 100.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
